event: content_block_delta
data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/tmp/mission-summary.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission m1 \\\"greet, tested\\\" — concluded: campaign-met\\n\\nAll three required criteria are satisfied:\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` defines `greet(name)` returning `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` covers a normal name and the empty string via\\n   `unittest`.\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0.\\n\\nWork was dispatched to `dev` (d002), who implemented both files and reported\\nsuccess on the first attempt. I did not take the report on faith: I fetched\\n`dev`'s branch, diffed it against the campaign base (confirming only the two\\nexpected files changed, nothing extra), read both files directly, and\\nre-ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself in a fresh, isolated\\ngit worktree at `dev`'s commit — independent of dev's own test run. It passed\\n(2 tests, OK, exit 0). Only then did I accept d002.\\n\\nI fast-forward merged dev's accepted commit onto my own branch\\n(`cs-sandbox/<ORCHESTRATOR:1>.<GROUP:1>`), which now sits at\\n`1928a68497e4aa5c492187fdac5f9cc0830a711f`, one commit ahead of the campaign\\nbase — the surviving record of this work. A full report and assessment were\\nwritten to the output directory.\\n\\nNothing remains unmet.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

